"""THE prediction artifact the manuscript reads from. One file, generated by gated code.

Everything the paper claims about an unmeasured compound must come from here. The reason this
script exists at all is that the eight predictions the project had been quoting lived only in a
scratchpad, while the one prediction file committed to the repo
(NOVEL_HALF_HEUSLER_PREDICTIONS.csv) issued four DIFFERENT compounds -- including ZrGaCu, HfGaCu
and HfInCu, which had already been refused three times for being hexagonal, and using stale
constants c=0.480 p=0.350 that contradict the calibration used everywhere else.

THE METHOD. For a compound that already has a published Boltzmann-transport calculation, no
machine learning is used or needed -- the calibrated calculation is measurably better than the ML
model (33.8% vs 42.1% median error, chemistry held out):

    kappa_measured = kappa_BTE x min(c (T/300)^p, 1)

(c, p) are refitted here from the current blind test every run. They are NOT constants: across one
day's runs c moved 0.37 -> 0.49 -> 0.54 as the reference set changed by under 2%, so the value is
written into every output row and must be quoted with its provenance.

ONE GLOBAL CALIBRATION, NOT PER-FAMILY. The (Y,Z) families demonstrably differ in their raw
BTE/measured ratio (Sb-Pd 3.45, Ni-Sb 1.69, Sb-Pt 1.43 against a pooled 1.81), and an adversarial
review argued each prediction family therefore needs its own factor. Tested nested in
family_cal_nested.py: leave-one-compound-out 35.8% -> 35.3% (p=0.09, better on only 22 of 51), and
under the standard leave-one-chemistry-out protocol the family factor is WORSE, 33.7% -> 35.3%.
Rejected. The families differ in sample; that difference does not transfer.

GATES, each from a real failure:
  VEC == 18            below/above it the compound is metallic and lattice kappa is not the story
  full BTE label       43% of tier-1 half-Heusler formulae carry a semi-empirical Slack label
  no actinide/radio    no 5f element appears anywhere in training
  proven (Y,Z) class   Bi-Pd is excluded outright -- 0% within 2x
  CUBIC ON RECORD      new here; a spacegroup 216/225 entry from a non-Alexandria source
  POLYMORPH FLAGGED    new here; a compound also recorded hexagonal cannot be issued as though
                       the cubic phase is the one that forms
  T-BASIS DECLARED     new here; a compound with a single 300 K BTE point cannot support a
                       200-1000 K curve, and the row says so rather than implying one
  IN-RANGE OF FAMILY   new here; see below

THE MASS-TREND RULE WAS WRONG AND IS NOT USED. The project had been flagging LaSbPt and GdSbPt as
"breaking the mass trend of their series" -- La being the lightest rare earth yet getting the
lowest kappa. Tested in scratchpad/trend_gate.py against the measured data: there is no mass trend
to break. Across six families with >=4 measured members the median Spearman(X-site mass, kappa) is
+0.200, the sign is inconsistent, and in Sb-Pt it is +0.949 (p=0.051) -- heavier gives HIGHER
kappa, the opposite of the supposed rule. Screening on it would have been an invented rule.

What replaces it is a claim that can actually be checked: a prediction landing OUTSIDE the range
its own family's measurements span is an extrapolation, and is flagged as one with the range
quoted. That catches the same two compounds for a reason that survives inspection, and it catches
ScSbPt as well, which the mass rule missed.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import glob
import json
import sys
import warnings

sys.path.insert(0, ".")
warnings.filterwarnings("ignore")
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from pymatgen.core import Composition, Element

import extend_blind_test as E
from run_loco_chemistry import cluster_of, sites

TRAIN = "data/Target_Materials/HEUSLER_KAPPA_TRAINING_SET.csv"
OUT = "data/Target_Materials/PAPER_PREDICTIONS.csv"
SP = "paper/evidence"
EXCLUDE_FAMILY = {"Bi-Pd"}
RADIO = {"U", "Ac", "Th", "Pa", "Np", "Pu", "Tc", "Pm", "Ra", "Po", "At", "Rn", "Fr"}
FLUCT = {"Ce": "4f valence fluctuation / Kondo -- kappa often anomalous",
         "Sm": "Sm can be di- or trivalent, so the VEC=18 assumption is unsafe",
         "Yb": "valence fluctuating", "Eu": "commonly divalent"}
SANITY = ("LaSbPd", "ScSbPd", "HoSbPd", "YSbPd", "ZrNiSn", "TiCoSb", "NbCoSn")
CUBIC = {216, 225}
_SG: dict = {}


def red(f):
    try:
        return Composition(str(f)).reduced_formula
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return None


def yzfam(f):
    try:
        e = sorted(Composition(str(f)).elements, key=lambda x: (x.X if x.X else 99.0))
        return f"{e[1]}-{e[2]}" if len(e) == 3 else None
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return None


def ve(e):
    gp = Element(str(e)).group
    return gp if gp <= 12 else gp - 10


def vec(f):
    try:
        return int(sum(ve(e) for e in Composition(f).elements))
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return None


def _load_spacegroups() -> None:
    """Every spacegroup on record per compound, from every NON-Alexandria structure source.

    Alexandria is a prototype-substitution database of hypothetical decorations: finding a
    compound there in spacegroup 216 says someone generated that decoration, not that the phase
    forms. Recording ALL spacegroups -- not merely whether a cubic one exists -- is what makes the
    polymorph check possible; DySbPd carries 186 and 194 alongside 216.
    """
    if _SG:
        return
    for path in glob.glob("data/external/*.csv"):
        if "alexandria" in path.lower():
            continue
        try:
            d = pd.read_csv(path, low_memory=False)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            continue
        fc = next((c for c in d.columns
                   if c in ("formula_reduced", "reduced_formula", "formula")), None)
        sc = next((c for c in d.columns
                   if "spacegroup_number" in c or c in ("space_group", "spacegroup", "sg")), None)
        if not fc or not sc:
            continue
        sg = pd.to_numeric(d[sc], errors="coerce")
        src = d.get("structure_source", pd.Series([""] * len(d))).astype(str)
        for f_, s_, o_ in zip(d[fc], sg, src):
            if pd.isna(s_) or "alexandria" in o_.lower():
                continue
            rr = red(f_)
            if rr:
                _SG.setdefault(rr, set()).add(int(s_))


def structure_status(r: str):
    """(has_cubic, also_non_cubic, sorted spacegroups on record)"""
    _load_spacegroups()
    sgs = sorted(_SG.get(r, set()))
    return bool(CUBIC & set(sgs)), bool([s for s in sgs if s not in CUBIC]), sgs


def measured_family_range(tr: pd.DataFrame) -> dict:
    """Room-temperature measured kappa span of each (Y,Z) family.

    A prediction outside its own family's measured span is an extrapolation -- the family has
    never been observed to reach that value -- and saying so is a checkable statement, unlike the
    mass-trend rule it replaces. Restricted to 250-350 K so the span is a like-for-like comparison
    with the 300 K prediction.
    """
    m = tr[(tr.tier == 0) & tr.k.notna() & (tr.k > 0) & tr["T"].between(250, 350)].copy()
    m["fam"] = m.formula.map(yzfam)
    out = {}
    for fam, sub in m.groupby("fam"):
        per = sub.groupby("red").k.median()
        if len(per) >= 3:
            out[fam] = (float(per.min()), float(per.max()), int(len(per)))
    return out


def main() -> int:
    bad = [c for c in SANITY if vec(c) != 18]
    assert not bad, f"VEC rule is broken -- known-good compounds not at 18: {bad}"
    print(f"VEC sanity check passed on {len(SANITY)} known-good compounds (all = 18)")

    g = pd.read_csv(f"{SP}/PROVEN_all_scored.csv")
    g["fam"] = g.compound.map(yzfam)
    ok = g[~g.broken & g.ratio.between(0.5, 2.0)]
    nok = ok.groupby("fam").compound.size()
    allf = g[~g.broken].groupby("fam").agg(err=("ape_med", "median"), bias=("ratio", "median"),
                                           n_all=("compound", "size"))
    PF = {f: (int(nok[f]), float(allf.err[f]), float(allf.bias[f]))
          for f in nok.index
          if nok[f] >= 2 and f not in EXCLUDE_FAMILY and f in allf.index}

    tr = pd.read_csv(TRAIN, low_memory=False)
    tr["red"] = tr.formula.map(red)
    tr["tier"] = pd.to_numeric(tr.method_tier, errors="coerce")
    tr["k"] = pd.to_numeric(tr.kappa_L, errors="coerce")
    tr["T"] = pd.to_numeric(tr.temperature_K, errors="coerce")
    measured = set(tr[tr.tier == 0].red.dropna())
    dft = tr[(tr.tier == 1) & tr.k.notna() & (tr.k > 0) & tr["T"].between(200, 1300)]
    dft = dft[~dft.red.isin(measured) & dft.red.notna()]
    dft = dft[dft.red.map(lambda r: (sites(r) or ["?"])[0] == "half")]
    dft = dft[dft.red.map(yzfam).isin(PF)]
    _m = dft.method.astype(str).str.lower()
    _not_bte = _m.str.contains("not bte|semi-empirical|slack|debye-callaway|reduced-model|"
                               "reduced model|bte-approx", regex=True)
    dft = dft[~_not_bte]

    # THE CALIBRATION MUST BE FITTED ON THE DOMAIN IT IS APPLIED TO.
    #
    # This previously fitted on every half Heusler in the blind test, including the thirteen that
    # fail the very screens applied below. The result was a prediction file carrying c=0.500,
    # p=0.950 while the manuscript's Methods and Results reported c=0.51, p=0.80 -- two different
    # calibrations for one method, which is the defect that got three earlier headline numbers
    # withdrawn. Every issued prediction now uses the same constants the paper reports.
    D = pd.read_csv("data/exports/kappa_v2/target_blind_test.csv")
    Dh = D[D.klass == "half"].copy()
    Dh["_vec"] = Dh.compound.map(vec)
    _sd = {x: structure_status(str(x)) for x in Dh.compound.unique()}
    Dh["_ind"] = (Dh._vec == 18) & Dh.compound.map(lambda x: _sd[x][0] and not _sd[x][1])
    dom = Dh[Dh._ind]
    print(f"calibration fitted on the {dom.compound.nunique()} in-domain compounds "
          f"(of {Dh.compound.nunique()} half Heuslers in the blind test)")
    c, p = E.fit_cp(dom)
    # CONFORMAL BANDS MUST COME FROM THE POPULATION THEY DESCRIBE.
    #
    # These previously read interval_validation.json's "half, support>=3" set: 46 compounds
    # including ones outside the declared domain, with residuals from a calibration fitted on the
    # pooled half AND full Heusler blind set -- which extend_blind_test.py's own docstring forbids.
    # The 90% band that produced was 3.21x where the in-domain validation supports 2.24x, so every
    # issued interval was about 43% wider than the evidence warrants. A band that is too wide is
    # not "conservative"; it is a coverage claim the method does not make.
    cf = json.load(open("data/exports/kappa_v2/conformal_indomain.json"))["levels"]
    b50 = cf["0.50"]["factor"]
    b90 = cf["0.90"]["factor"]
    print(f"calibration refitted now: kappa_meas = kappa_BTE x min({c:.3f}(T/300)^{p:+.3f}, 1)")
    print(f"bands (multiply/divide): {b50:.2f} at 50%, {b90:.2f} at 90%")
    FR = measured_family_range(tr)
    print("measured room-temperature span per family (the extrapolation reference):")
    for f_, (lo_, hi_, n_) in sorted(FR.items()):
        print(f"    {f_:<9}{lo_:>6.1f} - {hi_:<6.1f} W/m/K   from {n_} measured compounds")
    print()

    rows = []
    for r_, sub in dft.groupby("red"):
        fam = yzfam(r_)
        els = [str(e) for e in Composition(r_).elements]
        v = vec(r_)
        has_cubic, also_hex, sgs = structure_status(r_)
        rad = [e for e in els if e in RADIO]
        fl = [e for e in els if e in FLUCT]
        if rad:
            st, why = "REFUSED", f"radioactive/actinide ({','.join(rad)})"
        elif v != 18:
            st, why = "REFUSED", f"VEC {v} != 18 -- metallic, lattice kappa is not the whole story"
        elif not has_cubic:
            st, why = "REFUSED", ("no cubic Heusler (216/225) structure on record outside "
                                  f"prototype-decoration databases; spacegroups seen: {sgs}")
        elif also_hex:
            st, why = "FLAGGED", (f"POLYMORPHIC -- also recorded in "
                                  f"{[s for s in sgs if s not in CUBIC]}; the BTE label is for the "
                                  "cubic C1b phase, so the prediction holds only if that polymorph "
                                  "is the one synthesised")
        elif fl:
            st, why = "FLAGGED", FLUCT[fl[0]]
        else:
            st, why = "ISSUED", ""
        rec = dict(compound=r_, family=fam, VEC=v, status=st, note=why,
                   spacegroups=str(sgs), x_site=(cluster_of(r_) or "?").replace("half:X=", ""),
                   fam_verified=PF[fam][0], fam_err_pct=round(PF[fam][1], 1),
                   n_bte_rows=len(sub), n_bte_temps=int(sub["T"].nunique()),
                   c_used=round(c, 3), p_used=round(p, 3))
        for T in (300, 600, 900):
            near = sub.iloc[(sub["T"] - T).abs().values.argsort()[:1]]
            if not len(near) or abs(float(near["T"].iloc[0]) - T) > 150:
                rec[f"kappa_BTE_{T}"] = np.nan
                rec[f"kappa_pred_{T}"] = np.nan
                continue
            kd = float(near.k.iloc[0])
            rec[f"kappa_BTE_{T}"] = round(kd, 2)
            rec[f"kappa_pred_{T}"] = round(kd * min(c * (T / 300.0) ** p, 1.0), 2)
        if np.isfinite(rec.get("kappa_pred_300", np.nan)):
            k3 = rec["kappa_pred_300"]
            rec.update(lo50=round(k3 / b50, 2), hi50=round(k3 * b50, 2),
                       lo90=round(k3 / b90, 2), hi90=round(k3 * b90, 2))
        if rec["n_bte_temps"] <= 2:
            rec["T_dependence"] = (f"SINGLE POINT -- {rec['n_bte_temps']} BTE temperature(s); the "
                                   "600/900 K values carry the global exponent, not any "
                                   "compound-specific temperature dependence")
        else:
            rec["T_dependence"] = f"{rec['n_bte_temps']} BTE temperatures"

        # Extrapolation check -- runs last because it needs the predicted value.
        lohi = FR.get(fam)
        k3 = rec.get("kappa_pred_300", np.nan)
        rec["fam_measured_range"] = (f"{lohi[0]:.1f}-{lohi[1]:.1f} (n={lohi[2]})"
                                     if lohi else "no measured span")
        if lohi and np.isfinite(k3) and not (lohi[0] <= k3 <= lohi[1]):
            side = "below" if k3 < lohi[0] else "above"
            # HOW FAR outside matters, and the bare verdict hid it. Two compounds sat 9% past the
            # edge of a span built from five measurements, while another sat 139% past it -- the
            # same label for very different situations. Since two laboratories measuring the same
            # compound disagree by a median factor of 1.33, a 9% excursion is inside the noise of
            # the boundary itself. The margin is now recorded so a reader can judge, and a
            # refusal within the reproducibility factor is marked as marginal rather than clear.
            margin = k3 / lohi[1] if k3 > lohi[1] else lohi[0] / k3
            kind = "MARGINAL EXTRAPOLATION" if margin < 1.33 else "EXTRAPOLATION"
            extra = (f"{kind} -- predicted {k3:.2f} W/m/K falls {side} the "
                     f"{lohi[0]:.1f}-{lohi[1]:.1f} W/m/K span of the {lohi[2]} measured "
                     f"{fam} compounds by a factor of {margin:.2f}"
                     + ("; that is within the 1.33x median disagreement between laboratories, so "
                        "the boundary itself is not resolved at this precision"
                        if margin < 1.33 else
                        "; the family has never been observed there"))
            rec["extrapolation_margin"] = round(float(margin), 2)
            if rec["status"] == "ISSUED":
                rec["status"], rec["note"] = "FLAGGED", extra
            else:
                rec["note"] = f"{rec['note']} | {extra}"
        rows.append(rec)

    R = pd.DataFrame(rows)
    R = R[R.kappa_pred_300.notna()].sort_values(["status", "fam_err_pct", "compound"])
    R.to_csv(OUT, index=False)

    for st in ("ISSUED", "FLAGGED", "REFUSED"):
        s = R[R.status == st]
        print(f"=== {st}  ({len(s)}) ===")
        if not len(s):
            print("  (none)\n")
            continue
        if st == "ISSUED":
            print(f"  {'compound':<10}{'family':<8}{'BTE300':>8}{'pred300':>9}"
                  f"{'50% range':>14}{'90% range':>15}{'600K':>7}{'900K':>7}  T-basis")
            for r in s.itertuples():
                print(f"  {r.compound:<10}{r.family:<8}{r.kappa_BTE_300:>8.2f}"
                      f"{r.kappa_pred_300:>9.2f}{f'{r.lo50}-{r.hi50}':>14}"
                      f"{f'{r.lo90}-{r.hi90}':>15}"
                      f"{r.kappa_pred_600:>7.2f}{r.kappa_pred_900:>7.2f}"
                      f"  {'single 300K point' if r.n_bte_temps <= 2 else str(r.n_bte_temps) + ' temps'}")
        else:
            for r in s.itertuples():
                print(f"  {r.compound:<10}{r.family:<8} {r.note[:100]}")
        print()
    print(f"wrote {OUT}   ({len(R)} rows)")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
